00:02:16.768 --> 00:02:20.518
- [Voiceover] Dan asks, "Will
QR codes ever really take off?

00:02:21.175 --> 00:02:22.699
- This question because
of a couple of reasons.

00:02:22.699 --> 00:02:24.661
One, the answer's no; not in America.

00:02:24.661 --> 00:02:27.231
Two, I took the question because I want

00:02:27.231 --> 00:02:29.823
to give the VaynerNation 
the answer to why.

00:02:29.823 --> 00:02:32.165
The early use of QR codes in America,

00:02:32.165 --> 00:02:35.323
the first time we all saw
them, with smart-phones.

00:02:35.323 --> 00:02:37.411
Have you ever done a QR code, India?

00:02:37.411 --> 00:02:39.394
- I've never really used one
'cause they're all in subways,

00:02:39.394 --> 00:02:40.882
and I don't see what's
goin' on underground.

00:02:40.882 --> 00:02:42.934
- And, so, you've never done
it, you've never done it?

00:02:42.934 --> 00:02:44.383
Staphon, have you ever done it?

00:02:44.383 --> 00:02:45.460
For what?

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:47.743
- We had, back in the
business-card's days,

00:02:47.743 --> 00:02:49.715
we had, like, one of them.
- [Gary] Got it.

00:02:49.715 --> 00:02:52.379
So, what's interesting to me is

00:02:52.379 --> 00:02:54.204
it took off in South
Korea, and other places,

00:02:54.204 --> 00:02:57.666
where the early execution,
the first 10,000 executions

00:02:57.666 --> 00:03:00.602
of QR codes in that market, brought value.

00:03:00.602 --> 00:03:03.190
It was like you could buy
stuff from the subways.

00:03:03.190 --> 00:03:06.794
You could get really
interesting exclusive content.

00:03:06.794 --> 00:03:08.938
It was utilitarian, it was entertainment.

00:03:08.938 --> 00:03:11.503
In America marketers ruin everything.

00:03:11.503 --> 00:03:14.127
The very early QR codes
where you would take a shot

00:03:14.127 --> 00:03:15.721
of the QR code from a branding standpoint,

00:03:15.721 --> 00:03:19.449
the big-mass early stuff that
we saw a half-decade ago.

00:03:19.449 --> 00:03:21.521
And it would show people a YouTube video

00:03:21.521 --> 00:03:23.790
of the company's television commercial.

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:25.507
So, the reason QR codes won't take off

00:03:25.507 --> 00:03:28.770
is they've been branded,
ingrained in our collective heads,

00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:31.154
as something that doesn't
bring us any value.

00:03:31.154 --> 00:03:33.879
And, so, when something
establishes itself as not valuable,

00:03:33.879 --> 00:03:35.702
we don't go back to the well.

00:03:35.702 --> 00:03:38.588
If email started off as just
being spam and marketing,

00:03:38.588 --> 00:03:40.713
we wouldn't be there; and
it would have never evolved.

00:03:40.713 --> 00:03:43.932
At first it was a
communication social media.

00:03:43.932 --> 00:03:44.838
It's on, and on, and on.

00:03:44.838 --> 00:03:48.493
So, QR codes, unfortunate
for the QR code gods,

00:03:48.493 --> 00:03:50.872
did not have the right
execution in the US market.

00:03:50.872 --> 00:03:53.166
And the hill is too big
to climb, in my opinion,

00:03:53.166 --> 00:03:54.667
for it to really take off.

00:03:54.667 --> 00:03:57.530
Now, look, could a QR execution come along

00:03:57.530 --> 00:03:59.999
in the next 24 months that's so incredible

00:03:59.999 --> 00:04:02.740
and resets the value-prop around it,

00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:05.747
a la Snapchat, which they just did it.

00:04:05.747 --> 00:04:07.302
Let's throw up my Snapchat QR code

00:04:07.302 --> 00:04:08.524
so you can follow me, real quick.

00:04:08.524 --> 00:04:09.908
(camera snapping)

00:04:12.087 --> 00:04:14.543
You know, but even that I
think was really significant.

00:04:14.543 --> 00:04:17.125
The hottest app doing it,
a lot of us are doin' it.

00:04:17.125 --> 00:04:18.809
Maybe right now it's bubbling
up, and that will happen.

00:04:18.809 --> 00:04:20.635
So, it's not far-fetched
that it has a prayer

00:04:20.635 --> 00:04:22.675
based on something like that.

00:04:22.675 --> 00:04:24.019
But, in general, my prediction is no.

00:04:24.019 --> 00:04:25.275
And if somebody does something great,

00:04:25.275 --> 00:04:27.954
we'll replay this answer,
and I'll eat crow.

00:04:28.809 --> 00:04:30.724
- [Voiceover] Matt asks, "What's your take

00:04:30.724 --> 00:04:33.069
"on what Jay Z is trying to do with Tidal?

00:04:33.069 --> 00:04:35.008
"Do you think it will work?"

00:04:35.008 --> 00:04:37.772
- Matt, what Jay Z's doing with Tidal

00:04:37.772 --> 00:04:41.448
is what every good entrepreneur
that has brand-equity

00:04:41.448 --> 00:04:43.196
in the consumer marketplace
should be doing,

00:04:43.196 --> 00:04:44.729
which is leveraging 
their brand-equity

00:04:44.729 --> 00:04:46.992
to bring enormous 
exposure to something

00:04:46.992 --> 00:04:49.141
that they disproportionately
make more money on

00:04:49.141 --> 00:04:51.425
than if they were using
somebody else's thing.

00:04:51.425 --> 00:04:53.371
This is something that
should always be going on

00:04:53.371 --> 00:04:55.989
with the pseudo, triple
A-list celebrities.

00:04:55.989 --> 00:04:57.109
They should always own.

00:04:57.109 --> 00:04:59.467
They shouldn't be the face
of a cognac or a champagne;

00:04:59.467 --> 00:05:00.506
they should own it.

00:05:00.506 --> 00:05:03.992
They shouldn't be a face, or
beyond a platform for music;

00:05:03.992 --> 00:05:05.382
they should own it.

00:05:05.382 --> 00:05:07.010
Whether it's gonna work
or not; I have no idea.

00:05:07.010 --> 00:05:08.959
Because the truth is for me
to give a really good answer,

00:05:08.959 --> 00:05:10.404
I need to see the team that's actually

00:05:10.404 --> 00:05:12.159
executing the business.

00:05:12.159 --> 00:05:14.425
Just because you're a big-time star,

00:05:14.425 --> 00:05:17.186
and you bring your big-time
star friends along,

00:05:17.186 --> 00:05:18.787
doesn't mean the business
is gonna succeed.

00:05:18.787 --> 00:05:21.675
What's gonna happen is, if the operators,

00:05:21.675 --> 00:05:23.842
and I don't know what Hove
is doing with his time;

00:05:23.842 --> 00:05:26.198
but I have a feeling he won't
be the CEO of the platform.

00:05:26.198 --> 00:05:28.228
So, you know, he's an executor.

00:05:28.228 --> 00:05:29.931
The management team that's in place

00:05:29.931 --> 00:05:31.552
is going to determine its success.

00:05:31.552 --> 00:05:34.138
I have personal brand-equity
as a social media voice,

00:05:34.138 --> 00:05:35.897
at a very small level.

00:05:35.897 --> 00:05:37.405
Trust me, I'm not tryin'
to compare myself.

00:05:37.405 --> 00:05:39.860
But I was a triple, Z-list celebrity.

00:05:39.860 --> 00:05:41.631
But within a little world,
that social media world,

00:05:41.631 --> 00:05:42.821
I had a little equity.

00:05:42.821 --> 00:05:44.773
And if my management team, if I,

00:05:44.773 --> 00:05:47.215
if we didn't execute
properly, we would have lost.

00:05:47.215 --> 00:05:50.927
There's a lot of people that
use their fame as the match.

00:05:50.927 --> 00:05:51.743
(flesh smacking)

00:05:51.743 --> 00:05:54.258
The match, to make something happen.

00:05:55.093 --> 00:05:56.573
It all comes down to the steak.

00:05:56.573 --> 00:05:58.272
The sizzle's there, there's exposure.

00:05:58.272 --> 00:05:59.734
We all know what it is.

00:05:59.734 --> 00:06:01.072
There's a million music-services

00:06:01.072 --> 00:06:03.277
without a frontman like
Jay Z and the other people

00:06:03.277 --> 00:06:05.465
that we'd never know,
so, it's got a chance.

00:06:05.465 --> 00:06:06.327
A lot of people check it out.

00:06:06.327 --> 00:06:07.373
A lot of times it's not as strong

00:06:07.373 --> 00:06:08.677
as what's in the marketplace.

00:06:08.677 --> 00:06:11.746
Spotify, and Rdio, and
all these other things

00:06:11.746 --> 00:06:13.170
have big legs up.

00:06:13.170 --> 00:06:15.305
ITunes, they all have much longer time.

00:06:15.860 --> 00:06:17.107
So, they're further ahead.

00:06:17.107 --> 00:06:18.508
It's a marathon, not a sprint.

00:06:18.508 --> 00:06:19.727
I'm sure everybody's gonna write it off;

00:06:19.727 --> 00:06:22.131
people are already writing it off as dead.

00:06:22.131 --> 00:06:24.100
We'll see what happens over
the next 36 to 48 months.

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:25.561
A tremendous lesson for everybody

00:06:25.561 --> 00:06:27.942
watching and listening to the show is,

00:06:27.942 --> 00:06:29.081
it doesn't matter how you start;

00:06:29.081 --> 00:06:31.138
it matters how you finish.

00:06:31.138 --> 00:06:34.129
And, so, what I'm most curious about

00:06:34.129 --> 00:06:36.511
is that there's cynicism
around its success

00:06:36.511 --> 00:06:37.821
because a lot of famous people

00:06:37.821 --> 00:06:39.366
try to put their name on things.

00:06:39.366 --> 00:06:43.290
I look at Jay Z as a
uniquely strong businessman.

00:06:43.290 --> 00:06:44.838
If he's got infrastructure underneath him,

00:06:44.838 --> 00:06:46.778
or the right players
are in place for Tidal,

00:06:46.778 --> 00:06:48.316
in the next 24 to 48 months,

00:06:48.316 --> 00:06:50.292
it has a chance of doing something.

00:06:50.292 --> 00:06:52.263
I just don't know what's under the hood.

00:06:54.105 --> 00:06:56.073
Nice, I feel deep today.

00:06:56.073 --> 00:06:57.411
- [India] Yeah, it's very nice.

00:06:57.411 --> 00:06:58.913
- Good, India, glad you're excited.

00:06:58.913 --> 00:06:59.985
- [India] I'm real excited.

00:06:59.985 --> 00:07:02.652
- [Voiceover] Sally asks,
"Hey, Gary, did you decide

00:07:02.652 --> 00:07:04.136
"you wanted to sell your services

00:07:04.136 --> 00:07:06.629
"to the big Fortune 500 companies

00:07:06.629 --> 00:07:09.977
"versus consumers first, and
then come up with VaynerMedia?

00:07:09.977 --> 00:07:12.090
"In other words, did you
choose who you wanted

00:07:12.090 --> 00:07:14.404
"to sell to first to
maximize your profits,

00:07:14.404 --> 00:07:16.860
"and then come up with
the business model?"

00:07:17.785 --> 00:07:19.928
- Sally, this is an interesting question.

00:07:19.928 --> 00:07:23.374
I'm a very big fan of counter-punching;

00:07:23.374 --> 00:07:24.421
and a lot of people don't do that.

00:07:24.421 --> 00:07:25.632
A lot of people lay out business-plans,

00:07:25.632 --> 00:07:27.767
they know what they wanna
do, they go and do it.

00:07:27.767 --> 00:07:29.673
And they go raise money,
or they go and execute it;

00:07:29.673 --> 00:07:31.257
and that's how they roll.

00:07:31.257 --> 00:07:33.737
In lieu of the tremendous
fight this weekend,

00:07:33.737 --> 00:07:35.519
which it was boring to the masses;

00:07:35.519 --> 00:07:36.799
but it was a tremendous fight for me.

00:07:36.799 --> 00:07:39.183
Because 13 seconds in, I looked at AJ

00:07:39.183 --> 00:07:40.619
and said, "This fight's over."
'cause I couldn't believe

00:07:40.619 --> 00:07:43.513
how much faster Floyd was than Pacquiao.

00:07:43.513 --> 00:07:45.673
Watching them separately,
I thought it'd be closer.

00:07:45.673 --> 00:07:47.668
I knew a second that he
was that much faster,

00:07:47.668 --> 00:07:49.092
that the fight was over because I know

00:07:49.092 --> 00:07:51.311
what kind of defensive fighter Floyd is.

00:07:51.311 --> 00:07:53.356
And I'm sure everybody
gets bored and falls asleep

00:07:53.356 --> 00:07:54.656
rounds seven through 12,

00:07:54.656 --> 00:07:57.687
but I'm just completely,
like, infatuated with boxing.

00:07:57.687 --> 00:07:59.567
And understood the chess-moves
that he was playing out

00:07:59.567 --> 00:08:01.065
in the ring, and enjoyed that.

00:08:01.065 --> 00:08:03.005
Even though I know it's not commercially

00:08:03.005 --> 00:08:05.191
raw-raw, high-energy.

00:08:05.191 --> 00:08:07.746
I view myself very
similar as a businessman.

00:08:07.746 --> 00:08:11.970
I react, it's funny I feel
like I'm on full-offense.

00:08:11.970 --> 00:08:14.745
I feel like I'm 48-and-oh
because I play great defense

00:08:14.745 --> 00:08:16.167
based on reacting.

00:08:16.167 --> 00:08:18.419
I can react to the market and adjust.

00:08:18.419 --> 00:08:19.750
The market started coming to me

00:08:19.750 --> 00:08:21.475
because I was writing Crush It!,

00:08:21.475 --> 00:08:23.035
putting out videos around business,

00:08:23.035 --> 00:08:25.123
and amassing a large
social media following.

00:08:25.123 --> 00:08:26.939
And big-brands reached out to me and said,

00:08:26.939 --> 00:08:28.809
"Hey, you've got all these people."

00:08:28.809 --> 00:08:31.706
You know, "Hey, we're
a Fortune 500 company.

00:08:31.706 --> 00:08:33.176
"We have 50 followers on Twitter.

00:08:33.176 --> 00:08:34.974
"You have 400,000 followers!

00:08:34.974 --> 00:08:36.119
"We've never heard of you.

00:08:36.119 --> 00:08:37.582
"Come and teach us, come explain."

00:08:37.582 --> 00:08:38.768
Plus, moments in time.

00:08:38.768 --> 00:08:41.472
AJ was graduating from BU.

00:08:41.472 --> 00:08:43.153
It was time to do a business together.

00:08:43.153 --> 00:08:44.408
We were thinkin' about fantasy sports,

00:08:44.408 --> 00:08:45.634
we were thinkin' about other things.

00:08:45.634 --> 00:08:48.199
And, so, between timing
and being reactionary

00:08:48.199 --> 00:08:50.544
to the demand cycle,
like, we're responding

00:08:50.544 --> 00:08:52.438
to the demand that was in place.

00:08:52.438 --> 00:08:53.756
I was already playing in the space.

00:08:53.756 --> 00:08:55.420
It wasn't like I just made something up,

00:08:55.420 --> 00:08:57.318
or there was demand for
me out of left-field

00:08:57.318 --> 00:08:58.586
that made no sense.

00:08:59.496 --> 00:09:03.014
My actions were setting
up my counter-punch.

00:09:03.014 --> 00:09:05.137
My actions were setting
up my counter-punch.

00:09:05.137 --> 00:09:07.620
I think it's a very
strong model for business.

00:09:08.399 --> 00:09:09.885
- Hey, Gary, I'm Darius Nabors

00:09:09.885 --> 00:09:12.377
and I'm doing a 59-week,
59 National Park road-trip

00:09:12.377 --> 00:09:13.612
